; docs/semantics/kinds/os-rt.eadl — the `os/rt` workload feature module.
;
; ROADMAP.md §5.1: "Add workload requirements through an `os/rt` feature module." This file is
; that module, declared with the same `defkind` primitive as the core surface kinds — it is an
; extension, not a privileged addition.
;
; ⛔ WHAT A TASK MAY AND MAY NOT CARRY. §7.3 splits the task record in three, and only the first
; third lives here:
;
;   eADL                  the functional requirements and constraints
;   the build manifest    application-code association, execution bounds, and their evidence
;   the engine            concrete allocations, stack placement, and other implementation fields
;
; So a task declares its release behavior, its priority, its deadline and the event that
; deadline is relative to, its bounded jitter, and what it needs. It does NOT declare a worst-
; case execution time (that is evidence about a binary, valid only for a target and toolchain —
; §15 notes a compiler flag change invalidates it while the description is byte-identical), an
; entry point (a code association), or a stack size (an engine-derived allocation). Each of
; those three has a worked rejection case in `docs/semantics/boundary/reject/`.
;
; The profile bounds what is expressible here too: `rt-static-up-v1` admits "periodic or
; sporadic releases with declared minimum separation; constrained deadlines; bounded release
; jitter" and excludes `dynamic-task-creation`, so there is no clause for creating one.

(eadl-version eadl/1)

(defkind task
  (doc "a logical task: its release behavior, priority, deadline and functional needs")
  (name required)
  ; Release behavior. A periodic task states its period; a sporadic one states the minimum
  ; separation between releases. §3.1 admits both and requires the separation to be declared.
  ;
  ; ⛔ `(holds values quantity)`, not `(holds values number symbol)`. A symbol is a *name* and
  ; `parsec` is a perfectly good one, so the old declaration said nothing about whether the pair
  ; measured anything — which is how `archogen check` came to accept `(period 10 parsec)` while
  ; `archogen build` refused it. A quantity's unit is well-formedness and not behavior, so §5.6
  ; lets a kind declare it, and the refusal is `crates/eadl-model/src/quantity.rs`'s own
  ; diagnostic rather than a second code for the same mistake. Leaf `M1.28`.
  (clause period (cardinality at-most-one) (holds values quantity))
  (clause min-separation (cardinality at-most-one) (holds values quantity))
  ; §3.1: constrained deadlines. The reference event is explicit because §7.3 requires the
  ; "relative deadline and its reference event" — a deadline measured from an unnamed instant
  ; is not a checkable requirement.
  (clause deadline (cardinality one) (holds values quantity))
  (clause deadline-from (cardinality at-most-one) (holds values symbol))
  ; §3.1: bounded release jitter. Absent means zero, which is a claim the analysis relies on.
  (clause jitter (cardinality at-most-one) (holds values quantity))
  ; Static unique priorities (§3.1). The engine owns the ready-queue structure that realizes
  ; them; the number here is the policy, not the implementation.
  ;
  ; ⛔ THE NUMBER IS A RANK: a numerically LOWER value is a HIGHER priority, and 1 is the
  ; highest. See docs/decisions/decision_priority-comparison-direction.md. The direction was
  ; unrecorded until leaf S0.1 needed to order two releases at the same instant, and §15 forbids
  ; changing a parameter's comparison direction silently — so reversing it is a versioned
  ; language change with a migration note, never an edit here.
  (clause priority (cardinality one) (holds values integer))
  ; What the task requires of the system, functionally.
  (clause needs (cardinality any) (holds forms))
  (clause uses (cardinality any) (holds forms))
  ; §3.1: "defined overrun … policy". A named response, not a handler body. rt-static-up-v1
  ; performs `fault`, the default when the clause is absent, and `skip-late-job` (rule 5 of the
  ; fault contract, docs/profiles/rt-static-up-v1-faults.md; docs/semantics/model.md §4 rule 6); the checker
  ; refuses any other.
  (clause on-overrun (cardinality at-most-one) (holds values symbol)))
